problems, to make life easier for thousands of black motorists. State by State, he'd been putting together a travel guide with listings of restaurants, hotels, and service stations that would welcome African-American travelers. He called it the Negro Motorist Green book. The green book for short. Victor Green, who died in 1960, lived in Harlem, New York during the height of the Harlem Renaissance. His apartment was not far from Duke, Elingtons. His office would eventually be situated near smalls paradise, a famous nightclub. Victor didn't have the most obvious background for starting a travel guide. He didn't work in tourism, he wasn't a writer, he was a mailman in Hackensack, New Jersey.
问题，让成千上万的黑人驾车者生活得更轻松。他逐州整理了一份旅行指南，列出了欢迎非裔美国旅行者的餐馆、酒店和服务站。他称之为《黑人驾车者绿皮书》（The Negro Motorist Green Book），简称《绿皮书》。维克多·格林于1960年去世，他在哈莱姆文艺复兴时期住在纽约哈莱姆区。他的公寓离艾灵顿公爵（Duke Ellington）的住处不远。他的办公室最终设在著名的夜总会“斯莫尔天堂”（Small's Paradise）附近。维克多并没有从事旅行指南工作的明显背景。他不从事旅游业，也不是作家，他是新泽西州哈肯萨克的一名邮递员。

